---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../../components/Faq.astro';
import TrustBlock from '../../../components/TrustBlock.astro';
import { site } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'What Is eDPI? Effective DPI Explained for FPS Players';
const description =
  'eDPI = sensitivity × DPI. What it normalizes, why it is not the same as cm/360, and how to use both numbers to compare aim settings fairly.';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'What is eDPI?' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is eDPI the same as DPI?',
    a: 'No. DPI is a hardware property of the mouse; eDPI is DPI multiplied by in-game sensitivity, so the same mouse can produce any eDPI depending on the game slider.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I compare eDPI between different games?',
    a: 'Only loosely. eDPI ignores the game\'s yaw constant, so 800 eDPI in Valorant and 800 eDPI in CS2 are not the same physical sensitivity. Use cm/360 for cross-game comparison and eDPI for same-game comparison.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do all eDPI calculators give the same number?',
    a: 'Because the formula is trivial (sens × DPI). The value of a calculator is the surrounding context: bands, cm/360, and converting the number across games, which is what our tool adds.',
  },
];

const related = [
  { href: '/edpi-calculator/', title: 'eDPI Calculator', desc: 'Get your number plus cm/360 and band context.' },
  { href: '/guides/cm-360-explained/', title: 'cm/360 explained', desc: 'The cross-game metric that eDPI cannot replace.' },
  { href: '/sensitivity-converter/', title: 'Sensitivity Converter', desc: 'Apply matched aim across 13 games.' },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  canonical="/guides/what-is-edpi/"
  type="article"
  jsonLd={[
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'What Is eDPI? Effective DPI Explained for FPS Players',
      datePublished: '2026-10-07',
      dateModified: '2026-10-07',
      author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: site.name, url: `${site.url}/about/` },
    },
  ]}
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <h1>What is eDPI? Effective DPI explained</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      eDPI (effective DPI) is your in-game sensitivity multiplied by your mouse DPI. It removes DPI
      differences from comparisons, and nothing more. Here is what it is good for, and where it stops
      being useful.
    </p>

    <p class="small muted">Published 07 Oct 2026 · By the SensForge team</p>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="answer">Quick answer</h2>
      <p>
        <code>eDPI = in-game sensitivity × mouse DPI</code>. It lets two players with different DPI
        compare aim settings in one number. For example, 0.5 sens at 1600 DPI and 1.0 sens at 800 DPI are
        both 800 eDPI. Calculate yours with the <a href="/edpi-calculator/">eDPI calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="why">What eDPI actually normalizes</h2>
      <p>
        Because sensitivity and DPI multiply together, doubling one and halving the other keeps your
        effective movement per inch identical. eDPI collapses that pairing into a single figure, useful
        when discussing settings with someone who runs a different DPI step.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Player</th><th class="num">DPI</th><th class="num">Sens</th><th class="num">eDPI</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>A</td><td class="num">800</td><td class="num">1.0</td><td class="num">800</td></tr>
            <tr><td>B</td><td class="num">1600</td><td class="num">0.5</td><td class="num">800</td></tr>
            <tr><td>C</td><td class="num">400</td><td class="num">2.0</td><td class="num">800</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:10px">Same eDPI across three DPI steps, assuming raw input and no acceleration.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="limit">The catch: eDPI does not cross games</h2>
      <p>
        Each game converts mouse counts to rotation with its own yaw constant. eDPI contains no yaw term,
        so identical eDPI values produce different physical turns in different games. If you switch games,
        the number to preserve is <a href="/guides/cm-360-explained/">cm/360</a>, eDPI alone will mislead
        you. That is why our eDPI calculator shows both.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="use">How to use eDPI well</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Use it within a game</strong> to compare settings with teammates, quickly and fairly.</li>
        <li><strong>Pair it with cm/360</strong> whenever the conversation crosses games.</li>
        <li><strong>Treat bands as conventions.</strong> Most competitive players sit in 200–1,600 eDPI depending on title and role; outliers on both ends win tournaments.</li>
        <li><strong>Change one variable at a time</strong> if you experiment (DPI or sens, not both) so you know what caused the difference.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <Faq items={faq} />

    <TrustBlock>
      <p class="mb-0">
        Formula is arithmetic; no empirical claims are made on this page. For yaw sources used in the
        cross-game conversion, see the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.
      </p>
    </TrustBlock>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="next">Next steps</h2>
      <ul class="related-grid" style="list-style:none;padding:0">
        {related.map((r) => (
          <li><a class="tool-card" href={r.href}><span class="tc-title">{r.title}</span><p class="tc-desc">{r.desc}</p></a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
